  0.93, 0.97, 0.93],ExpressionUUID->"c3c09a5e-c0a1-48fb-8c69-b8e21517f746"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["7. Two statistical laws of semantic change", "Section",ExpressionUUID->"cd061a39-f0b1-4bd1-b6bb-1c1a22eb78a9"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "Hamilton, Leskovec & Jurafsky (2016) propose two regularities of semantic \
",
 "change. Both can be tested directly on the aligned embeddings, measured \
over the ",
 StyleBox["11332",
  FontWeight->Bold],
 " dictionary content words present in every decade (function ",
 "words and archaic/foreign tokens removed via a stoplist and ",
 StyleBox["WordData",
  FontFamily->"Courier"],
 " membership). A word's ",
 StyleBox["rate of change",
  FontSlant->Italic],
 " is the mean cosine distance ",
 "between its aligned vectors in consecutive decades; its ",
 StyleBox["frequency",
  FontSlant->Italic],
 " is proxied by its mean rank, and its ",
 StyleBox["polysemy",
  FontSlant->Italic],
 " by its number of WordNet senses. Re-deriving the laws independently is \
also a good ",
 "way to watch a confound masquerade as a result. (Statistics: ",
 StyleBox["deLawFacts[]",
  FontFamily->"Courier"],
 ".)"
}], "Text",
 CellMargins->{{50, 50}, {6, 
  6}},ExpressionUUID->"b31a9fad-79ed-4aba-aa7c-c3df74e9e424"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["The law of conformity \[Dash] holds", "Subsection",ExpressionUUID->"8c518abb-4ba5-4484-9803-8cb23da5427b"],

Cell[TextData[{
 StyleBox["More frequent words change more slowly.",
  FontWeight->Bold],
 " Change-rate falls steeply ",
 "with frequency (Spearman \[Rho] = ",
 "-0.94",
 ", slope ",
 "-0.12",
 " in log-rate). ",
 StyleBox["Why:",
  FontSlant->Italic],
 " a high-frequency word is seen ",
 "in a huge range of contexts, so its vector is an average over many uses and \
is ",
 "statistically well-anchored. Part of the strength is also methodological \
\[Dash] rare ",
 "words are estimated from little data, so their vectors are noisier and ",
 StyleBox["look",
  FontSlant->Italic],
 " like they move more \[Dash] which is itself worth stating plainly."
}], "Text",
 CellMargins->{{50, 50}, {6, 
  6}},ExpressionUUID->"3b0ad575-4a9b-4593-9a01-35233fdc89af"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{"deConformityPlot", "[", "]"}]], "Input",
 CellMargins->{{50, 30}, {8, 8}},
 FontSize->12,
 CellLabel->" In[7]:=",ExpressionUUID->"4c378764-993d-44eb-a9e3-a35bea6e1a1c"],

Cell[OutputFormData["\<\
-Graphics-\
\>"], "Output",
 CellChangeTimes->{3.989148236205665*^9},
 CellLabel->" Out[7]=",ExpressionUUID->"edb448ed-338c-49b0-94eb-81e4c98f099f"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[TextData[{
 StyleBox["Figure. ",
  FontWeight->Bold],
 "Rate of semantic change vs. frequency (\[Minus]log mean rank). Red ",
 "markers are decile means."
}], "Caption",
 CellMargins->{{55, 55}, {2, 
  16}},ExpressionUUID->"118ea385-04f5-44e0-8e9f-5bf56ae853a3"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["The law of innovation \[Dash] a cautionary tale", "Subsection",ExpressionUUID->"c9300dad-caf4-4598-94f2-118a781fbd2c"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "The published law says ",
 StyleBox["more polysemous words change faster",
  FontWeight->Bold],
 ". In this corpus the ",
 StyleBox["raw",
  FontSlant->Italic],
 " correlation comes out the wrong way: change-",
 "rate vs. polysemy has Spearman \[Rho] = ",
 "-0.31",
 " \[Dash] negative. The culprit is a ",
 StyleBox["confound",
  FontWeight->Bold],
 ": polysemous words tend to ",
 "be frequent (\[Rho] = ",
 "0.34",
 "), and frequency strongly suppresses change. In the raw comparison the \
frequency ",
 "effect simply dominates \[Dash] a textbook Simpson's paradox."
}], "Text",
 CellMargins->{{50, 50}, {6, 
  6}},ExpressionUUID->"2822bdf4-cdbc-43df-812e-b4420eea5470"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "Controlling for frequency (partial Spearman correlation, holding frequency ",
 "rank fixed) the spurious negative trend vanishes \[Dash] but it does not \
turn ",
 "convincingly positive either: partial \[Rho] = ",
 "0.01",
 ", essentially zero. ",
 StyleBox["So in the Google-N-grams eng-all corpus, with WordNet sense counts \
as the polysemy measure, the law of innovation does not replicate.",
  FontWeight->Bold],
 " That is a more honest \[Dash] and more useful \[Dash] result than forcing \
the sign: ",
 "the law may need a different corpus, a better polysemy measure, or the \
original ",
 "authors' frequency-and-polysemy-matched controls to surface."
}], "Text",
 CellMargins->{{50, 50}, {6, 
  6}},ExpressionUUID->"6be1b58c-e76a-42df-9e9c-6bee72b27309"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{"deInnovationPlot", "[", "]"}]], "Input",
 CellMargins->{{50, 30}, {8, 8}},
 FontSize->12,
 CellLabel->" In[8]:=",ExpressionUUID->"1472cff6-eb89-487f-9dac-66f9af6c8811"],

Cell[OutputFormData["\<\
                                                             7
Legended[-Graphics-, Placed[LineLegend[{Directive[PointSize[---], 
                                                            360
 
>       AbsoluteThickness[2], RGBColor[0.560181, 0.691569, 0.194885], 
 
                                                7
>       Thickness[Large]], Directive[PointSize[---], AbsoluteThickness[2], 
                                               360
 
>       RGBColor[1, 0, 0], Thickness[Large]]}, 
 
>     {raw mean rate, frequency-controlled (residual)}, 
 
>     LegendMarkers -> {{-Graphics-, Offset[9]}, {-Graphics-, Offset[9]}}, 
 
>     Joined -> {True, True}, LabelStyle -> {}, LegendLayout -> Column], 
 
>    {0.7, 0.78}, Identity]]\
\>"], "Output",
 CellChangeTimes->{3.989148236227017*^9},
 CellLabel->" Out[8]=",ExpressionUUID->"4efe8a4b-c950-4be1-a298-d5aa658e4e62"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[TextData[{
 StyleBox["Figure. ",
  FontWeight->Bold],
 "The raw polysemy trend (blue) slopes the \"wrong\" way only because ",
 "polysemous words are frequent; the frequency-controlled residual (red) is \
flat. Raw ",
 "Spearman ",
 "-0.31",
 ", partial ",
 "0.01",
 "."
}], "Caption",
 CellMargins->{{55, 55}, {2, 
  16}},ExpressionUUID->"40df1e89-0463-4ef4-9c8a-5609914a85c5"],

Cell[TextData[{
 StyleBox["Takeaway.  ",
  FontWeight->Bold],
 "Conformity replicates cleanly; innovation does not survive a frequency ",
 "control here. A large raw correlation is easy to get and easy to misread \
\[Dash] ",
 "always ask what else moves with your variable before naming an effect."
}], "Text",
 CellFrame->{{3, 0}, {0, 0}},
 CellMargins->{{60, 60}, {8, 12}},
 CellFrameColor->RGBColor[0.35, 0.6, 0.4],
 FontSize->12,
 Background->RGBColor[
  0.93, 0.97, 0.93],ExpressionUUID->"75d0057e-3362-4c08-a89a-807c8511f90d"]
}, Open  ]]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["8. The most-drifted words \[Dash] and a caveat", "Section",ExpressionUUID->"4ed41fa5-1f87-4ce0-b82b-8b8cdf93ccb6"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "Ranking every stable content word by how far it moved between its 1800s and \
",
 "1990s aligned vectors surfaces two phenomena at once. Some entries are \
genuine meaning ",
 "change (",
 StyleBox["advert",
  FontSlant->Italic],
 " verb \[Rule] noun, ",
 StyleBox["peer",
  FontSlant->Italic],
 " noble \[Rule] equal, ",
 StyleBox["media",
  FontSlant->Italic],
 ", ",
 StyleBox["broadcast",
  FontSlant->Italic],
 "); others are lexical obsolescence or register shift, where an archaic word \
(",
 StyleBox["twelvemonth, fourthly, civilities",
  FontSlant->Italic],
 ") simply stopped occurring in the same ",
 "kinds of text. A large embedding displacement means ",
 "\[OpenCurlyDoubleQuote]the contexts changed\[CloseCurlyDoubleQuote] \[Dash] \
which is ",
 "necessary, but not sufficient, for \[OpenCurlyDoubleQuote]the meaning ",
 "changed\[CloseCurlyDoubleQuote]. Distinguishing the two is where embeddings \
end and ",
 "lexicography begins."
}], "Text",
 CellMargins->{{50, 50}, {6, 
  6}},ExpressionUUID->"6b38591c-8572-48bb-9abd-8269138099ba"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{"deDriftChart", "[", "25", "]"}]], "Input",
 CellMargins->{{50, 30}, {8, 8}},
 FontSize->12,
 CellLabel->" In[9]:=",ExpressionUUID->"9324c2f8-b8b1-4497-af5f-82af9a50968e"],

Cell[OutputFormData["\<\
-Graphics-\
\>"], "Output",
 CellChangeTimes->{3.989148236412298*^9},
 CellLabel->" Out[9]=",ExpressionUUID->"3bcd8118-e8ad-4c35-a668-c6feb5135a1d"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[TextData[{
 StyleBox["Figure. ",
  FontWeight->Bold],
 "The 25 most-drifted stable content words, 1800s\[Dash]1990s. The full ",
 "ranking is in ",
 StyleBox["data/drift_ranking.json",
  FontFamily->"Courier"],
 "."
}], "Caption",
 CellMargins->{{55, 55}, {2, 
  16}},ExpressionUUID->"16b07934-8222-4152-aba2-af195372a73e"],

Cell[TextData[{
 StyleBox["Takeaway.  ",
  FontWeight->Bold],
 "Embeddings are a powerful detector of ",
 StyleBox["that",
  FontSlant->Italic],
 " something changed about a word's usage; deciding ",
 StyleBox["what",
  FontSlant->Italic],
 " changed still needs the neighbour lists, the trajectories, and human \
judgement."
}], "Text",
 CellFrame->{{3, 0}, {0, 0}},
 CellMargins->{{60, 60}, {8, 12}},
 CellFrameColor->RGBColor[0.35, 0.6, 0.4],
 FontSize->12,
 Background->RGBColor[
  0.93, 0.97, 0.93],ExpressionUUID->"1cedbc1d-1fc2-4a59-991e-00d64196c1d2"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["9. Methods and reproduction", "Section",ExpressionUUID->"47003837-f959-42c8-8a94-acbdfbe37dab"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "All notebook code is in ",
 StyleBox["wolfram/DiachronicEmbeddings.wl",
  FontFamily->"Courier"],
 "; each output above is a single call to one of its functions, drawing from \
the small ",
 "committed data products in ",
 StyleBox["data/",
  FontFamily->"Courier"],
 ". To regenerate those products from ",
 "scratch (the science is pure Wolfram Language; the only Python is a \
NumPy/pickle ",
 "format shim):"
}], "Text",
 CellMargins->{{50, 50}, {6, 
  6}},ExpressionUUID->"c7b77b6e-f6cd-4d21-b18d-8ec373e2e313"],

Cell[TextData[{
 StyleBox["wolframscript -file wolfram/fetch_data.wls",
  FontFamily->"Courier"],
 "  \[Dash] download + unzip the HistWords eng-all SGNS embeddings"
}], "Item",
 CellMargins->{{66, 50}, {3, 
  3}},ExpressionUUID->"38363e19-2864-4bd1-8ec7-21e7cbf6598e"],

Cell[TextData[{
 StyleBox["python3 src/convert_histwords.py",
  FontFamily->"Courier"],
 "  \[Dash] .npy/.pkl \[Rule] float32 binaries + vocab (anchors, targets, \
pools)"
}], "Item",
 CellMargins->{{66, 50}, {3, 
  3}},ExpressionUUID->"1a8b59eb-3f6f-48fe-b5eb-98f3a1e22d77"],

Cell[TextData[{
 StyleBox["wolframscript -file wolfram/trajectories.wls",
  FontFamily->"Courier"],
 "  \[Dash] Procrustes alignment, projection \[Rule] trajectories.json + \
neighbors.json"
}], "Item",
 CellMargins->{{66, 50}, {3, 
  3}},ExpressionUUID->"c24f2e25-6ef8-45d0-99bd-89a486e065f9"],

Cell[TextData[{
 StyleBox["wolframscript -file wolfram/laws_and_drift.wls",
  FontFamily->"Courier"],
 "  \[Dash] drift leaderboard + the two laws \[Rule] laws.json, \
laws_points.json, drift_ranking.json"
}], "Item",
 CellMargins->{{66, 50}, {3, 
  3}},ExpressionUUID->"9578067b-dda1-4ead-bf05-e945890a2b04"],

Cell[TextData[{
 StyleBox["wolframscript -file wolfram/alignment.wls",
  FontFamily->"Courier"],
 "  \[Dash] the alignment diagnostic \[Rule] alignment.json"
}], "Item",
 CellMargins->{{66, 50}, {3, 
  3}},ExpressionUUID->"18bc834e-0311-4cf6-a699-a01373cebd35"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "Repository: ",
 StyleBox["github.com/mthiel74/DiachronicWordEmbeddings",
  FontFamily->"Courier"],
 "."
}], "Text",
 CellMargins->{{50, 50}, {6, 
  6}},ExpressionUUID->"74889d8f-9d6f-40c2-90cd-fedb2a908701"]
}, Open  ]],

